versions.analysis_for_next:
Version 0 sends far too many neutral comments to negative: 136 of 320 neutral texts, against 160 kept neutral. In this dataset annotators reserve negative for a clear personal emotion: despair, fear or sadness, insults and curses, or the claim that the numbers are lies. Many other comments look critical but are labelled neutral:
- demands and orders (close borders, test more, publish villages, bring back measures, resign);
- 'why' questions to the institute;
- suggestions;
- scolding careless citizens or cafes;
- sarcasm about testing ('no tests, no cases');
- 'league table' jokes about cities being on top;
- 'ma gjall' cheering of numbers.

Neutral-to-positive errors (24) come from wishes or relief that the annotators treat as neutral, such as 'Dhasht Zoti me pak raste' (God grant fewer cases), 'Edhe pak e iku virusi' (a little more and the virus is gone) and 'Prishtina n Top form' (Prishtina in top form). Positive itself is noisy, though, so I only add that sports-style jokes about cities leading the numbers are not positive.

The main change is to make neutral the default and to raise the bar for negative: criticism, demands or complaints phrased as advice, a question or a joke stay neutral. Negative still keeps curses, lie accusations, explicit sadness or fear, and despair about Kosovo. I keep the rule that praise mixed with criticism counts as positive.

Version 2 swung too far toward negative: 158 neutrals were called negative, and macro-F1 fell to 67.8. Version 1, with only 15 such errors but 88 negatives called neutral, scored best at 72.0. The model responds sharply to wording, so version 3 needs a deliberate middle line.

New neutral errors in version 2 mostly came from texts with only implicit criticism:
- demands for lockdowns or borders;
- requests for villages, ages or data;
- 'why' questions;
- comparisons with Italy, Germany or Albania;
- sarcasm about low testing;
- scolding citizens who skip masks;
- city 'league' and 'jackpot' banter;
- name tags and gibberish.
The broad cues 'mockery', 'sarcasm' and 'boll' (enough) pushed these into negative.

Negatives that version 1 missed carried an explicit marker:
- blame or fault words: faj (fault), qeveria koti (the Kurti government, used mockingly);
- claims that the numbers are invented: aparati (the machine), copy paste, 5000 numra (5000 numbers);
- explicit fed-up words: bajat (stale), lodhet (tired), merzit (fed up), spo na intereson (we don't care);
- insults: injoranta (ignorant), pisa (dirty one), ju raft (may it hit you);
- fear or despair: katastrof (catastrophe), tmerr (horror), deshtim (failure).

Plan: make neutral the default for requests, proposals, questions, comparisons and banter, even when they are critical or sarcastic. Require negative to show an explicit hostility or distress marker: blame, a fabrication claim, an insult, a fed-up statement or fear. Keep the positive criterion from version 2, which recalled 85 of 109 positives.

Same plan as before, with the wording trimmed to stay under 400 words:
- restrict approving words next to name tags to explicit approval, not nicknames or slang;
- restore 'praise mixed with criticism stays positive';
- note that praising the Kurti government is positive.
